  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186060928,
      "id": "33a00498-88d9-5e17-b4f5-8e716af98f79",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "To take the **transpose of a matrix**, we interchange each of its columns with the corresponding row. That is, row $1$ becomes column $1,$ row $2$ becomes column $2,$ and so on. A superscript ${}^{T}$ is used to denote the transpose operation.\n\nFor example, the transpose of the $2 \\times 3$ matrix ${X}$ is the following $3 \\times 2$ matrix $X^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{X} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}-4}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{blue}-3} & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{X}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t{\\color{red}7} & {\\color{blue}-3}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{blue}1}  \\\\\n        \t\t{\\color{red}-4} & {\\color{blue}0}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first row of $X$ becomes the first column of $X^T,$ and the second row of $X$ becomes the second column of $X^T.$\n\nNote that transposing a matrix swaps its dimensions. In general, we have the following property:\n\n> *When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $m \\times n$ to $n \\times m.$*\n\nAlso, notice that the transpose of a column vector is a row vector:\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t5  \\\\\n        \t\t4  \\\\\n                        2 \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n       \\qquad \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t5 & 4 & 2\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the transpose of a row vector is a column vector:\n\n$$\n{B} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t8 & -10 & 0 & 6\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n        \\qquad\\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{B}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t8  \\\\\n                        -10  \\\\\n                        0  \\\\\n                        6\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "2979",
      "stepId": "17592186060641",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186059552,
      "id": "ccf834be-e5b6-5030-a26a-f64e38613cf8",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186059743,
        "title": "Finding the Transpose of a Matrix"
      },
      "markdown": "Find $C^T,$ if \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4386",
      "stepId": "17592186060642",
      "title": "Finding the Transpose of a Matrix",
      "workedSolution": "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n\n$$\nC^T =\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186059553,
      "id": "8b0c4fc2-5f5e-58c2-8e6c-38e03f031b30",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186059744,
        "title": "Finding the Transpose of a Row Vector or Column Vector"
      },
      "markdown": "If\n    \n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        5 & -1 & 0  \\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthen what is ${A^T} ?$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4387",
      "stepId": "17592186060643",
      "title": "Finding the Transpose of a Row Vector or Column Vector",
      "workedSolution": "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n    5  \\\\\n    -1  \\\\\n    0  \\end{bmatrix}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186059554,
      "id": "4d99ce4c-edc1-5cd1-ae67-77c0b48b0298",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186059745,
        "title": "Determining the Dimensions of the Transpose of a Matrix"
      },
      "markdown": "Consider the matrix\n    \n\n$$\n{B} = \n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        1 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\\n        4 & 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\\n        3 & -3 & 0 & -1\n    \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^T?$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-4388",
      "stepId": "17592186060644",
      "title": "Determining the Dimensions of the Transpose of a Matrix",
      "workedSolution": "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $N \\times M$ to $M \\times N.$\n\nSo, since $B$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix, the transpose ${B}^T$ is a $4\\times3$ matrix."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186060929,
      "id": "9401cd1d-7957-54b6-b983-acc495088e54",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Remember that transposing a matrix swaps the rows and columns. So, if $B=A^T,$ then $b_{ij}=a_{ji}$ for all values of $i$ and $j.$ \n\nGeometrically, when a matrix is transposed, the diagonal elements (those with $i=j$) remain the same while all other elements are reflected across the diagonal.  For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\qquad  \\implies\\qquad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso,  transposing a matrix twice results in the original matrix. More precisely, for any matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( A^T\\right)^T = A.\n$$\n\nThis happens because, in the first transpose, we swap the rows of $A$ for the columns, and then in the second transpose, we swap them back again. For example,\n\n$$\n{A} = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6}\n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & 1  \\\\\n        \t\t\\color{blue}{7} & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix}\n\n      \\quad  \\implies\\quad\n\n\t\\left( {A}^T\\right)^T = \n    \t\t\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \t\t\\color{red}{3} & \\color{blue}{7}  \\\\\n        \t\t1 & \\color{red}{6} \n    \t\t\\end{bmatrix} =A.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any numbers $c_1,c_2$ and any $m \\times n$ matrices $A,B$ we have the following rule:\n\n$$\n(c_1 A \\pm c_2 B)^T = c_1 A^T \\pm c_2 B^T\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "2980",
      "stepId": "17592186060645",
      "title": "Properties of the Transpose of a Matrix"
    }
  ],
